```c
ret = btrfs_reserve_extent(root, len, len, fs_info->sectorsize,
                           0, alloc_hint, &ins, 1, 1);
if (ret == -EAGAIN) {
```
Reason:
The `btrfs_reserve_extent()` function returns `-EAGAIN` when there are no active zones available for allocation, and this error is not handled properly in the direct I/O path. As a result, the error propagates to userland, causing the "Resource temporarily unavailable" error.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (ret == -EAGAIN) {
    ASSERT(btrfs_is_zoned(fs_info));
    wait_on_bit_io(&inode->root->fs_info->flags, BTRFS_FS_NEED_ZONE_FINISH,
                   TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE);
    goto again;
}
```
Reason:
These statements implement the missing logic for handling the `-EAGAIN` error in the direct I/O path. By waiting for ongoing I/O to finish before retrying the allocation, the code ensures that the allocation can proceed once an active zone becomes available, thus preventing the error from reaching userland.

